                    caption: "Early Hemodynamic TAV Deterioration Treated by ViV With a SAPIEN X  Case #1: A 77-year-old woman with prior mechanical mitral valve replacement underwent transcatheter aortic valve replacement with a 23-mm SAPIEN for severe aortic valve stenosis 3 years ago and was recently readmitted for acute pulmonary edema. (A, B, and D) TTE showed moderate-to-severe transvalvular regurgitation, no. leaflet tear, (C) but some degree of leaflet thickening. (E) Continuous-wave Doppler showed a mean gradient of 17 mm Hg. (F to H) Noncontrast CT showed no evidence of leaflet mineralization. Contrast CT was not performed (hemodynamic instability/renal failure); therefore, the diagnosis of leaflet thrombosis cannot be formally excluded. A ViV was performed with a 23-mm SAPIEN XT. (I, J) Cinefluoroscopy and (K) TTE showed the stent frame of the new TAV (green dashed lines and arrow) implanted in a relatively high position compared with the stent of the prior TAV (blue dashed lines and arrow). The MG remained similar to the MG observed (L) before the procedure and there was only a trace of residual regurgitation. CT = computed tomography; AO = aorta; LA = left atrium; LV = left ventricle; RV = right ventricle; TTE = transthoracic echocardiography; ViV = valve-in-valve."
