                    caption: "Process of 3D simulation and printing for AVR fistula closure. A,B: Anatomical segmentation and 3D rendering of relevant cardiac anatomy, TAVR valve and ARV.C:Planning 3D printing where structures in red are printed with resin to allow for a higher resolution and structures in gray with filament as they do not require such high resolution and it allows to obtain larger printing volumes at reduces printing times.D,E: Simple rendering (D) and final 3D printed model (E) examining the fistula from the right ventricle.F: Fluoroscopic simulation of the procedure with the placement of an Amplatzer device in the AVR (arrowhead). ARV, aorta to right ventricle; TAVR, transcatheter aortic valve replacement.  Figure 2 Generation of three-dimensional (3D) biomodel of aortic-to-right ventricular fistula. (A to B) Three-dimensional rendering evidencing the Iocation of the ARV fistula, TAVR valve, and relevant cardiac anatomy. (C to D) Three-dimensional computer model used for 3D printing. (E to F) Three-dimensional printed model and physical procedure simulation with the placement of an Amplatzer occluder in the ARV fistula (arrowhead). ARV, aorta to right ventricle; TAVR, transcatheter aortic valve replacement; ARV, aorta to right ventricle; TAVR, transcatheter aortic valve replacement."
